WEBVTT

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:06.360
Whoops!

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.320
Over here!

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.870
Now that we're done with the year-end cleanup,

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.120
all that's left to do now is wait to ring in the new year.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.370
Since the house is so spacious,
cleaning it was a bit of a toil.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.040
Sorry about that.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.500
I owe you big time for helping out.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.920
No, no. Think nothing of it!

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:32.880
After New Year's Day,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.050
all the stores around here will be closed for a while,

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.600
so we'd better get our shopping done now.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.270
You may count on me to carry it home!

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:56.010
<i>MOUNT MIYAMA SHOPPING TOWN</i>

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.680
Sorry, they just caught my eye.

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:10.560
Well, there is still time until dinner.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:12.020
Yes.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:13.820
It's delicious, Shirou.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:23.330
Shirou...

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.500
Doesn't it seem more crowded than usual?

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:28.580
Yeah, I think you're right.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.960
They're probably here for the same reason as us.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.670
So they're here to prepare for New Year's?

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:40.550
What's the matter, Saber?

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:42.760
This New Year's holiday...

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.180
What exactly do you do?

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:48.640
I guess you could say it's an
event to welcome the new year.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.820
Welcome! Come one, come all!

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.820
We put up decorative pine trees,
like the one I made the other day,

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.030
and set out rice cake offerings.

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:59.740
Oh, and one more thing... We also eat osechi dishes.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:01.740
Osechi?

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.370
<i>It's delicious, you know!</i>

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:03.990
I know about osechi.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:07.040
Taiga told me all about it the other day.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:10.250
It's a diverse array of dishes
packed in multi-tiered boxes.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:13.250
<i>OSECHI
Taking Reservations</i>

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:11.670
That's what you mean, right?

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:12.880
Right.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.760
What you put inside,
like beans, herring roe or fish cake...

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.590
They each have their own symbolic meaning.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:22.930
For example,

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.220
in the case of beans,

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:27.430
you eat them hoping for
health so you can work diligently.

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:28.850
Who knew...

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.440
Then it's fitting for a New Year's celebration.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.360
I'd love to try it.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:35.690
Yeah.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:37.320
We're having osechi tomorrow.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.740
But before we do that,

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:41.200
on the menu for tonight...

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.450
<i>SHINSHU SOBA</i>

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:43.780
is this!

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:46.580
We're home!

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.790
Hey, Shirou!

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.790
Hey, Shirou!

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.250
Welcome home!

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.250
Welcome home!

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.880
Where did this kotatsu come from?

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:59.300
Oh, I brought it over.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.430
You both look pretty incapacitated.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.640
I didn't set up a kotatsu
because I could see this happening!

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:10.890
Shirou, once you get in,
you can never, ever get out again!

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:14.440
It's like you're under some kind of spell.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:16.150
You, too, Illya?

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:19.070
Fuji-nee, we're back from shopping.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:20.440
Give me a hand, will you?

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:25.660
Sorry, but Big Sister will be
busy tomorrow, making the rounds.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:28.950
Today's my day to conserve my stamina.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:30.120
Oh, but you watch!

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:32.370
I'm going to be unstoppable on the 1st!

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:33.750
Why?

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.250
The 1st is the one day you really shine, Fuji-nee.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.920
One day? Hey, what do you mean, one day?

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:42.840
Taiga in a kimono, huh?

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:44.680
That's something I'd like to see.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:48.100
Oh, I know! Want to try one on, too, Saber?

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:49.310
You, too, Illya. Remember?

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.810
Kimonos, just like old times!

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:55.940
The clothes from that time...
they weren't half bad, you know.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.990
Saber, there's something I want you to help me with...

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.740
Oh, so Saber got swallowed up by the kotatsu, too, huh?

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:29.680
This is the only thing on the menu today.

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:33.680
I made the soup broth in advance, so we're good.

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:39.150
Soup broth is what you use as
the base for your noodle broth.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:41.820
You fill a saucepan with sweet rice wine.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.400
Bring it to a boil to let the alcohol evaporate.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:45.950
Turn down the heat,

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:47.950
add sugar and cook until it dissolves.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:54.250
Add soy sauce and heat it at a
low simmer, so it doesn't burn.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.580
Once it starts bubbling around the rim of the saucepan,

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:00.630
and you see white foam rising to the surface,

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.550
turn off the heat and skim off the foam.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.010
Set it aside to cool, and your soup broth is done.

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:13.140
Next, the kelp I left to soak since yesterday...

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:16.270
You combine water and kelp, leaving it to soak,

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:20.560
pour it into a saucepan, then remove
the kelp just before it comes to a boil.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.990
After lowering the heat, add bonito flakes,

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.030
and simmer for 30 to 40 minutes.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:33.830
Later, strain it with a straining
cloth, and you'll have your kelp broth!

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.330
The leeks should be chopped finely.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.840
The mustard spinach should be
boiled, then cut into bite-size slices.

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:48.260
The fish cake should be sliced
into eight-millimeter chunks.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:53.760
And now the tempura...

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:02.400
Remove the shell and digestive tract from the shrimp,

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:06.690
then cut off the tip of the tail to remove any liquid.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:12.620
Then rub and rinse the shrimp
with a little salt, potato starch and sake.

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:16.950
Next, prepare the batter.

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:20.210
Mix the eggs thoroughly with cold water.

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:23.420
Then after adding flour and stirring,

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:25.340
coat the shrimp with the batter,

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:29.010
and deep-fry them at 170 to 180 degrees Celsius.

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:35.680
Little more! Come here!

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:57.410
Now, combine the broth with
the soup broth, and season to taste.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:01.210
After boiling the soba noodles al dente,

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:03.960
rinse them under cold running water, then drain them.

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:08.210
Ladle the broth onto the noodles
you've heated up in hot water,

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:12.840
and your shrimp tempura is done!

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:14.970
Mustard spinach...

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:16.100
Fish cake...

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:17.810
Japanese leeks...

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.520
And citron rinds are your garnish...

00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:34.410
All right!

00:08:36.530 --> 00:08:38.410
Here you go! Toshikoshi soba!

00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:42.830
It wouldn't be New Year's Eve without this!

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:45.170
Toshikoshi soba?

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.420
Is it different than regular soba?

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:48.590
Yeah.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:53.470
Does this have some kind of
symbolic meaning, too, like osechi?

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:57.010
Since soba cuts easier than other noodles,

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:59.600
we eat it to cut off this year's bad fortune.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:02.180
And since we can stretch it long and thin,

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:05.350
I guess it's like a good luck
charm for health and longevity.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:06.560
I see.

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:07.940
Oh...

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:10.320
All right, then.

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:13.610
Let's partake of this so that we can all be healthy.

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:19.120
Thank you for this meal!

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:37.300
Two whole shrimps? I feel so spoiled!

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:40.850
You know, I really like this soup.

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:11.880
I'm planning to go to Ryudou Temple after this.

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:15.510
If you're going, Shirou, then I shall accompany you.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:17.510
You coming, Illya and Fuji-nee?

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:20.180
I don't want to leave this kotatsu!

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:21.890
I'll stay at home.

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:26.890
Tomorrow's my big day.
I mean, I'll be visiting Ryudou Temple.

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:28.690
You two go on, all right?

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:31.400
See you later!

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:39.410
Is Issei gonna be okay?

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:42.620
I mean, it's gonna be total chaos over New Year's...

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:44.330
You mean Ryudou Temple?

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:45.330
Yeah.

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:47.710
There's gonna be a huge swarm of worshippers.

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:53.670
<i>RECEPTION</i>

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:49.540
Worship?

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:53.250
Is there a proper etiquette for worshipping?

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:56.050
l guess you could say that.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:58.720
But you know what, it's not that hard.

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:03.350
Then if you could advise me,
I shall acquit myself flawlessly.

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:08.730
Saber...

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:14.440
There are all kinds of symbolic
reasons to eat toshikoshi soba.

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:17.530
And one of them is...

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:18.990
Yes?

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:27.580
"So that we can be near each other forever."

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:35.590
There's... Uh, I mean...

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:37.590
That one just came to mind.

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:39.300
Um...

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:41.510
You know... What I mean is...

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:46.560
Best wishes for next year, Saber.

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:49.520
Yes...

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:52.440
Shirou.
